{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"331f7cc2651d7ebc9fa55d53119b40661c21debfe6902c099b1503221ecdbc4d","slug":"results.task-structure.task-structure-overview","location":"/results/task_structure/task_structure_overview.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Representation of the Task Structure","content_includes_title":false,"keywords":["Uncertainty","Quantification","Reinforcement Learning","Bayesian Inference","Active Inference"],"numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"task_structure_overview.md","url":"/tambi-website/_public/task_structure_overv-bd155dcfc5be914c851b64d46c337374.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The analyses in this part address how participants represent the structure of the task and how they distribute their defence across the possibilities they consider. The task contains three kinds of structure that a participant can represent or ignore. The first is the set of possible attack directions, namely two latent states per color cue, about 120° apart, and a third direction 120° from both that is never the direction of a latent state but can be the direction of an oddball. The second is the temporal structure, in which the latent state persists for an episode of variable length and is interrupted by single displaced attacks (oddballs). The third is the coupling of the two color cues, whose attack directions are offset by about 70° and change state together.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"ziolrMhLno"}],"key":"sBrK9DgQOf"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A single displaced attack is ambiguous on the first placement after it (t+1), because an oddball and an episode transition present the same evidence, an attack about 120° from the expected direction. The second placement after the event (t+2) is the first at which the two can be told apart, because it follows the first attack after the event, which returns to the previous direction after an oddball and stays at the new direction after a transition. The response to the event at t+1 and t+2, and the time a participant needs before placing the nets, therefore show how the participant handles an ambiguity that the task structure resolves only on the next trial.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"JikOvAf1On"}],"key":"n1UmQx1p6i"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The analyses are organized in four chapters.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"iEoNgqAEVM"}],"key":"c4oAfwDpLE"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/results/task-structure/strategies-and-durations","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responses to Events and Placement Duration","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"VZ5bkprWye"}],"urlSource":"strategies_and_durations.md","dataUrl":"/results.task-structure.strategies-and-durations.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key":"oQvBOcSss9"},{"type":"text","value":" classifies the placement at t+1 and t+2 into strategies (followed the displaced attack, moved part of the way, nets on both directions, stayed, moved the other way, spread) and relates them, together with the placement duration at the event trial (t0), t+1 and t+2, to instruction condition and questionnaire scores.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"O8yjm6ZhuU"}],"key":"ubvtqOeWSD"}],"key":"NVyG1CM6RU"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/results/task-structure/structure-use","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use of the Two-State and Temporal Structure","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"bOr9snC1sJ"}],"urlSource":"structure_use.md","dataUrl":"/results.task-structure.structure-use.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key":"jp8LHGz3zY"},{"type":"text","value":" asks whether participants treat the direction of the other latent state differently from the direction that is never a state, whether their response to a displaced attack depends on the time since the last episode transition, how many placements they need before all nets are at the new direction after a transition, and how these measures change over the session.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"r2jvRaUhKK"}],"key":"whB838QRwP"}],"key":"HbRIN9RHAC"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/results/task-structure/replayed-uncertainty","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replayed State Uncertainty and Behaviour","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"RCNTKa39sO"}],"urlSource":"replayed_uncertainty.md","dataUrl":"/results.task-structure.replayed-uncertainty.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key":"xnH7qpwmqo"},{"type":"text","value":" relates the trial-by-trial uncertainty of the fitted latent-state model about the active state to the placement duration, the spread of the nets and the placement of nets on both directions, and to the questionnaire scores.","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"CExzbn4YTt"}],"key":"Xfpl0wlqrW"}],"key":"YHWb8x53kT"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/results/task-structure/task-structure-interpretation","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interpretation","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"wMaSz7msii"}],"urlSource":"task_structure_interpretation.md","dataUrl":"/results.task-structure.task-structure-interpretation.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key":"M51oYY2Llb"},{"type":"text","value":" relates these results to each other and to the results by research question.","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"eWv3dSES6s"}],"key":"HotTKqHVdJ"}],"key":"wJjcrMWOIy"}],"key":"cZMu1aOb8v"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"All results refer to run ","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"DXaTRIEmIx"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"13_11","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"Mq4BqZo631"},{"type":"text","value":" (86 participants with behavioural data, 85 of them with questionnaire data; None, 31; Partial, 33; Full, 22). The instruction condition is coded throughout with the planned contrasts Full versus None and Partial and Partial versus None (see ","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"dSwcvA6Cfv"},{"type":"link","url":"/results/results-by-rq","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Results by Research Question","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"WtJRQ86ogR"}],"urlSource":"../results_by_RQ.md","dataUrl":"/results.results-by-rq.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key":"WfATYndwMA"},{"type":"text","value":", section 1.5). The questionnaire analyses use the five questionnaires of the main analyses, the State-Trait Inventory for Cognitive and Somatic Anxiety (STICSA; state and trait scale), the Intolerance of Uncertainty Scale (IU), the Beck Depression Inventory-II (BDI) and the Obsessive-Compulsive Inventory-Revised (OCI), each standardized across participants, with the Benjamini–Hochberg correction (q) across the five questionnaires for each model term unless stated otherwise. The analyses of the response strategies and of the post-task questions additionally report the five further questionnaires of the battery, corrected separately from the five core questionnaires.","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"nNEuSfOsIS"}],"key":"WgwRq9r2mV"},{"type":"table","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Analysis","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"PSy3vZzTff"}],"key":"QhQoOVpQv2"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Script (pipeline step)","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"WmxsmU6Kh1"}],"key":"BOCRmOU6dK"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","